<section id="base">
		<section>
			<h1>Semantics</h1>
		</section>

		<section>
			<p>The structure of the page should be available to software (not only visual). </p>
		</section>
				
		<section>
			<h2>Language of page</h2>
			<pre><code is:raw data-trim>&lt;html lang="en"&gt;</code></pre>
			<aside class="notes">the lang attribute has to be present in the html tag. It allows a screenreader to read the page with the proper pronunciation.  </aside>
		</section>	
		<section>
			<h2>Language of text fragment</h2>
			<p>Only if language of fragment &lt;&gt; language of page </p>
			<pre><code is:raw data-trim>&lt;blockquote lang="en"&gt;
	I'm gonna make him an offer he can't refuse.
&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>
 			<aside class="notes">The lang attribute should be added only to content that is not in the main language of the page. It can be added to most HTML tags. </aside>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h2>Example: list of press releases in different languages</h2>
			<p><img class="stretch" src="https://images.anysurfer.be/language/ombudsman_press.png" alt=""></p>
		</section>
	
		<section>
			<h2>Semantic markup</h2>
			<p>Use the most suitable HTML tags:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Headings: <code is:raw>h1</code>-<code is:raw>h6</code></li>
				<li>Lists: <code is:raw>ul, ol, li </code></li>
				<li>Tables: <code is:raw>table, caption, tr, th, td</code></li>
				<li>Landmarks: <code is:raw>header, nav, main, footer</code></li>
			</ul>
			<aside class="notes"><p> The structure of a page should not only be obvious visually but also when looking at the code. It should be available for assistive technology, to create a table of contents automatically or enable shortcuts.</p></aside>
		</section>
		<section>
			<h2>Headings</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>Text that looks like a heading is marked as headings</li>
				<li>Not every big or bold text is a heading</li>
				<li>A heading is usually followed by some related content</li>
				<li>Choose proper heading level to create a logical hierarchy</li>
			</ul>
			  <aside class="notes"><p>If one produced a table of contents based on the headings found on the page, it should reflect the structure of the page. </p>
                                <p>It's OK to skip a heading level but not to invert heading levels. The subheading of a h3 cannot be a h2.  </p></aside>
			
		</section>
	
<section>
<h2>How to check heading levels?</h2>
<ul>
<li>Web developer toolbar: outline headings</li>
<li>Check 'Show Element Tag Names' </li>
<li>Or deactivate CSS, or apply custom stylesheet.</li>
</ul>
</section>

